  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nMCS that tracked out of Iowa earlier today intensified again as\nit crossed the Peoria metro, producing winds around 70 mph and\nextensive damage in Bloomington. Cluster of storms has reached\nthe Champaign area this hour, with the trailing outflow\nextending west through Springfield. Storms are riding along a\ntight gradient of CAPE and should exit the forecast area in the\nnext few hours.\n\nNext MCS is expected to begin developing this evening closer to\nthe warm front from Iowa to northern Indiana, then sink\nsouthward overnight. Precipitable water values over 2 inches \npersist through the night, and some of the northern parts of our\nforecast area have seen a few inches of rain since yesterday. \nHREF PMM precip estimates suggest 1.5-2 inches of rain in 6 \nhours between midnight and sunrise, and no changes are being \nmade to the Flood Watch at this time. South half of the forecast\narea has a good chance of remaining dry until closer to \ndaybreak.\n\nHeat forecasts remain a concern as well. Latest guidance\nsuggests 100-105 heat index could reach as far north as a\nRushville-Paris line tomorrow, but the extent of the lingering\nrain/cloud shield introduces some uncertainty as to whether or\nnot to expand the Heat Advisory. Current inclination is to leave\nit in place and allow the next shift to expand it, once the\ntiming and track of the MCS becomes more certain. An expansion\nfor the entire forecast area appears more likely for Saturday. \n\nA quick look at the extended forecast -- upper pattern becomes\nmore zonal for a time as high pressure sprawls more west-east\nacross the southern U.S. Getting into early next week, riding\namplifies west of the Rockies as a strong upper trough swings\nacross the Canadian Prairies and the north central U.S. Impacts\nfrom that in our area would be more likely toward the middle of\nnext week. A couple days of cooler temperatures are likely \nearly next week, before warming begins again.",
